# Normalise line endings in the repository, and check them out as LF everywhere.
#
# This is not cosmetic. A shell script checked out with CRLF fails on Linux and macOS
# with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M", which is exactly what would happen to the git
# hooks below if a Windows clone committed them back with CRLF. The repository is
# developed on Windows and runs its CI on three platforms, so the ambiguity is real
# rather than hypothetical.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must never be touched.
#
# `* text=auto` above asks git to guess, and git guesses correctly on these: it inspects
# the content and leaves anything it reads as binary alone. Saying so explicitly anyway,
# because the cost is one line and the failure mode is a Windows checkout quietly
# rewriting bytes inside a compiled resource, which then produces an executable with a
# corrupt icon and no error anywhere.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.mp4 binary
*.ncry binary
*.ico binary
*.syso binary
*.exe binary

# Line-ending conventions shared across the wider set of repositories this one belongs to.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
# CRLF on purpose for these two families: a PowerShell script or a batch file with LF
# endings can misbehave under older Windows shells, and unlike the Unix case the failure
# is not a clean error message.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
